    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Ward gates, curfew drums and the court gazette",
    text: "Chang'an was divided into 108 walled wards, each with its gates, streets, temples and a ward head responsible for order. At dusk drums sounded from the palace and along the avenues, the ward gates shut, and anyone caught on the main streets after dark could be beaten; at dawn the drums opened them again. Inside a ward, households were grouped in fours and fives who answered for one another's taxes and crimes. News moved through the wards by gossip, posted edicts and travellers at the markets. Officials read a court gazette copied by hand; a fragment of the Kaiyuan-era bulletin survives, and later Chinese tradition counted it among the world's first newspapers.",
    wiki: ["Chang'an", "Dibao", "Tang dynasty"],
