    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Tax cloth and hand mills: women's work in a Tang household",
    text: "The Tang tax system made every farmhouse a textile workshop. Each adult man owed grain, labour and a quota of silk or hemp cloth, and it was the women of the household who reared silkworms, reeled, spun and wove to meet it, on top of cooking, fetching water and caring for children and the old. Grain was ground in rotary hand mills or pounded in foot-operated mortars; wealthier households had water mills, which monasteries and officials ran for profit. Wheat flour made steamed cakes and noodles, new foods of the age. A Dunhuang manuscript records women forming associations to share the cost of weddings and funerals, a sign of how their labour tied households together.",
