    text: "A Tang house in the capital stood inside a ward: rammed-earth walls, a gate to the lane, rooms around a courtyard, tiled roofs for the prosperous and thatch for the poor. The law fixed how many bays and gates an official of each rank might build, and commoners were limited to three bays. Roofs needed re-tiling and walls re-plastering after summer rains. Moving was not free: households were registered where they lived, and leaving without permission made you a runaway. Yet many did, fleeing tax or famine to settle as tenants elsewhere; in the 720s the official Yuwen Rong sent inspectors to register some eight hundred thousand such households where they now lived.",
