    title: "The Hanjia granary and a household's grain jars",
    text: "The Tang state stored grain on an enormous scale. The Hanjia granary at Luoyang held hundreds of underground pits, each lined with fired clay, wood and matting and able to hold tens of tonnes; one excavated pit still contained carbonised millet. Ever-normal granaries bought grain cheap after harvest and sold it cheap in dearth, and charity granaries in each prefecture were filled by a levy on landholders for famine relief. At home, a family kept its millet, wheat and beans in large jars and baskets, raised off the damp floor and sealed against rats. Checking the store meant judging how far it would stretch until the summer wheat.",
    wiki: ["Luoyang", "Tang dynasty"],
