    text: "Tang law required a marriage to be arranged by the heads of both families through a matchmaker, and made the acceptance of betrothal gifts, often silk, binding; a family that backed out after that could be punished. Great clans of the north-east were so prized as in-laws that the court tried to stop them marrying only among themselves. Couples were expected to build a household together and produce sons, but the law also allowed divorce by mutual consent. Dunhuang manuscripts preserve model divorce letters that speak of a marriage where the two were 'like a cat and a rat' and wish the wife a better husband and a long life.",
