    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Loan contracts from Turfan and the Inexhaustible Treasury",
    text: "Tang households in trouble borrowed grain in spring and repaid after harvest, often at high interest, and the dry tombs of Astana at Turfan have preserved the contracts: named borrower and lender, amount, due date, interest, witnesses, and a clause that if the borrower fled, his wife and sons must pay. Officials, merchants and monasteries all lent. The most famous lender was the Inexhaustible Treasury of the Three Stages sect at Huadu temple in Chang'an, which took donations and lent them out without formal contracts until the emperor Xuanzong confiscated it in 713. Tang law capped interest and forbade seizing more than the debt, but enforcement fell to the local magistrate.",
