    text: "Ordinary worship in early imperial China centred on the she, the altar of the local earth god, where each village held feasts in spring and autumn, sacrificing a pig and sharing wine and meat; Tang poets describe villagers staggering home drunk from the autumn she. Inside the house, the stove god watched the family and reported on it. Buddhist devotion added new patrons: in the Tang, the trade associations of Youzhou, rice dealers, butchers and silk merchants among them, paid for Buddhist sutras to be carved on stone slabs at Yunju temple, and their names survive on the stones. Craftsmen and merchants sought protection for their work in the same way.",
