    title: "Village heads, the magistrate and the Tang Code",
    text: "A quarrel between neighbours in early imperial China was meant to be settled low down: by kin, by the village head (lizheng) and elders, who knew both parties and preferred a compromise. If it went further, the county magistrate heard it in person, sitting in his hall, questioning plaintiff and defendant, and he could beat either with a light or heavy stick. The Tang Code of 653 set out five grades of punishment and precise penalties for fights, depending on the weapon used and whether blood was drawn. It also punished false accusation and jumping levels of appeal, which gave everyone a reason to think twice before going to court.",
