    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Buffalo, treadle pumps and tenant plots from Song to Qing",
    text: "From the Song onward, Chinese farming grew ever more intensive. In the rice south a family worked a few mu of paddy with a water buffalo, a curved-beam plough and a harrow, lifted water with a chain pump worked by foot or by an ox, and transplanted seedlings by hand; double-cropping rice or rice and wheat was normal in the lower Yangzi by the Ming. In the north, oxen, mules and donkeys pulled ploughs through wheat, millet and sorghum. Most farmers were tenants or owner-tenants paying half the main crop as rent. Animals were few and precious; pigs and chickens lived on scraps and supplied manure, and there were almost no pastures outside the steppe frontier.",
    wiki: ["Chain pump", "Agriculture in China", "Water buffalo"],
