    text: "By late imperial times little true wilderness remained in the Chinese heartland, and what was left was worked hard. On Lake Tai and the Yangzi, fishing families lived their whole lives on boats, marrying among themselves; in the south the Tanka boat people were barred from the examinations and from settling ashore. Farmers raised carp in ponds, and in the Pearl River delta combined fish ponds with mulberry dykes whose silkworm droppings fed the fish. Fry netted in the Yangzi at Jiujiang were shipped across the south. Upland people cut bamboo, gathered mushrooms and medicinal roots and burned charcoal. Hunting was a frontier pursuit, though Qing bannermen hunted each autumn at the imperial Mulan grounds.",
