    text: "Song China burned coal on a scale unmatched until early modern Britain. A writer of the 1120s noted that the millions of households of Kaifeng all used coal and none firewood, and iron works in Hebei and Henan consumed coal and charcoal to produce, by one estimate, over a hundred thousand tonnes of iron a year in the 1070s. Mines were sunk as shafts with windlasses and drained by hand. Under the Ming, Zunhua and Foshan were great iron centres, and Beijing burned coal from the Western Hills around Mentougou, carried in by camel. The Qing drew copper for coin from the mines of Yunnan, where tens of thousands of miners worked under merchant contractors.",
