    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Night markets, paper money and periodic fairs",
    text: "The Song swept away the old walled markets and curfews. Shops opened onto every street of Kaifeng and Hangzhou, restaurants and night markets ran past midnight, and the Qingming scroll shows stalls, teahouses and hawkers crowding the bridges. Sichuan merchants invented paper notes, the jiaozi, which the state took over in 1024. Over the following centuries a network of periodic markets spread across the countryside, meeting every few days in rotation, so that a farmer could walk to one and back in a day, selling eggs and cloth and buying salt, iron and oil. Barter never vanished: villagers traded grain for cloth or labour, and copper cash was often short.",
    wiki: ["Along the River During the Qingming Festival", "Jiaozi (currency)", "Economy of the Song dynasty"],
