    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Tribute grain boats, wheelbarrows and porters' gangs",
    text: "The largest carrying job in late imperial China was the tribute grain: millions of piculs of southern rice shipped each year up the Grand Canal to feed the capital and its garrisons, first at Kaifeng, then at Beijing, on thousands of barges hauled through locks by trackers on the towpaths. On land, goods moved on shoulder poles, single-wheeled barrows that could carry a man or several hundred kilograms, mule carts on the dry northern roads, and porters' backs on the mountain paths of the south-west. In every city, porters and dockworkers organised into gangs that controlled particular docks, streets or gates, and fought off outsiders who tried to take their loads.",
    wiki: ["Grand Canal (China)", "Wheelbarrow"],
