    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The county yamen: magistrate, clerks and runners",
    text: "A late imperial county of two or three hundred thousand people had one centrally appointed magistrate, an outsider barred from serving in his home province and moved every few years. He governed from the yamen, a walled compound of halls and offices, with private secretaries he paid himself, and hundreds of clerks and runners who were local men. The clerks kept tax registers and legal papers; the runners delivered summonses, collected taxes, guarded prisoners and escorted the magistrate. Both were poorly paid or unpaid and lived on fees, so the yamen had a reputation for extortion. Villagers dealt with the state mostly through these men and through their own headmen.",
