    text: "The ideal of late imperial China divided work by the house wall: men ploughed outside, women wove inside. In reality women also transplanted rice, picked cotton and tended silkworms, but their core domain was the inner quarters: cooking on a brick stove, feeding pigs, sewing and mending, raising children, and above all spinning and weaving cotton, often late into the night by lamplight, for sale as well as for family use. Foot-binding, which spread from the elite to many commoner families by the Ming and Qing, kept girls at spinning wheels and looms from an early age. The daughter-in-law served her mother-in-law until she ran the kitchen herself.",
