    title: "Heirs, adoption and the pressure on a young couple",
    text: "A married couple in late imperial China built its future around one question: would there be a son to continue the line and make offerings to the ancestors? Mencius was quoted to say that of the unfilial acts, having no heir was the worst. Couples prayed to Guanyin, the Giver of Sons, and to local goddesses, and consulted doctors. If no son came, the law and lineage rules allowed a man to adopt a nephew or other boy of the same surname as heir, a formal act written into genealogies, and wealthier men took concubines. Girls were sometimes given away as future daughters-in-law, raised in the household they would marry into.",
