    text: "Chinese history after the Song is a history of migration. When the Jurchen took Kaifeng in 1127, perhaps millions fled south to the Yangzi. The early Ming moved whole populations to repopulate the war-ravaged north; many northern families still say their ancestors set out from under the great pagoda tree at Hongdong in Shanxi. After the wars of the seventeenth century emptied Sichuan, the Qing encouraged settlers from Hubei, Hunan and Guangdong, a movement remembered as 'Huguang fills Sichuan'. Migrants carried their dialects and gods with them, built native-place halls in their new towns, and faced years of clearing land, borrowing seed and quarrelling with earlier settlers.",
