    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Mediation first, the magistrate last",
    text: "Late imperial Chinese villagers were told to avoid the courts, and mostly did. A quarrel over a boundary, a debt, water rights or a slight was first taken to kin, then to lineage elders, a village head or respected neighbours, who met the parties over tea or a meal and pushed for a compromise in which neither lost face. The early Ming even ordered village elders to hear minor disputes. If mediation failed, one side wrote or paid a scribe to write a plaint to the county magistrate. Qing archives show courts were in fact busy with land and debt cases, and magistrates often sent them back to mediators. Republican courts after 1912 kept the same habit.",
