    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Primers and village schools in Song China",
    text: "Printing and the expanded examinations made the Song a schooling age. Boys from families with any ambition were sent at six or seven to a village school or a teacher hired by several households, where they memorised the Hundred Family Surnames and the Thousand Character Classic, and later the Classic of Filial Piety and the Analects, chanting aloud and copying characters with a brush. Lineages and local officials founded charity schools for poor kin. The Three Character Classic, written late in the Song, became the standard first primer for the next seven centuries. Daughters of educated families were often taught at home, and Song writers argue over how much learning a girl should have.",
