    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Rented rooms and fire towers in Song Kaifeng",
    text: "Kaifeng around 1100 held perhaps a million people, crammed into timber-framed houses along lanes that no longer had ward walls. Many rented; the government itself was a landlord through an office that let out state-owned houses and set rents. Fire was the constant danger. The city had watchtowers with soldiers on lookout, fire stations in every district with buckets, ladders, axes and ropes, and flags to signal where a blaze was. Repairs meant fresh plaster on walls of wattle and earth, re-laying roof tiles after storms, and replacing rotten timbers. After Kaifeng fell, Hangzhou suffered repeated great fires, and rebuilding after one was a common reason for debt.",
