    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Canal water, alum and rice-straw fuel in the south",
    text: "In the canal towns of the lower Yangzi and the south, water was everywhere but not always clean. Households drew it from canals by stone steps, let it settle in big glazed jars, and cleared it with a lump of alum stirred through; wells and rain water collected from roofs into jars were preferred for drinking and tea, and water from famous springs was sold. The same canals carried sewage and washing, so water was boiled. Fuel was the real shortage. The hills near Suzhou and Hangzhou had long been stripped, so farmers burned rice straw, bean stalks, reeds and grass, and towns bought firewood and charcoal brought in by boat from the uplands.",
    wiki: ["Suzhou", "Alum", "Grand Canal (China)"],
