    topics: [{ workplace: "field" }, { workplace: "pasture" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Small farms, shared oxen and the Buck surveys",
    text: "The farm economy of Republican China is unusually well measured, because John Lossing Buck and his students at Nanking University surveyed some seventeen thousand farms in the 1920s and 1930s. The typical farm was small, around three to four acres, split into scattered plots, and worked almost entirely by human labour; many families owned no draft animal and borrowed or hired an ox or buffalo for ploughing. Pigs and chickens were near universal, fed on scraps. Around Shanghai, farmers turned to cotton, vegetables and eggs for the city market. Rents, taxes levied years in advance by warlords, floods and falling crop prices in the Depression squeezed tenants and owners alike.",
