    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Fishing junks, boat dwellers and famine foods",
    text: "Fishing fleets from the Zhoushan islands off Ningbo worked the East China Sea in sailing junks, chasing the spring runs of yellow croaker and selling to Shanghai's fish markets through wholesale brokers who often held them in debt. On the rivers and creeks, families lived on sampans, fishing with nets and cormorants, carrying goods and dredging mud for fertiliser. The truly wild mattered most in disaster: in the famines of north China in the 1920s, and during war and flood in the 1930s and 1940s, villagers stripped elm bark, dug roots, ate chaff, wild greens and even clay to survive, and relief workers photographed whole villages gathering them.",
    wiki: ["Zhoushan", "Boat Dwellers", "Famine food"],
