    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Mills, presses and lane workshops of Shanghai",
    text: "By the 1930s Shanghai held about half of China's modern industry: cotton mills owned by Chinese, Japanese and British firms, silk filatures, flour mills, cigarette factories, shipyards and the Commercial Press, the country's largest publisher. Mill hands, many of them young women from Subei and the villages around the city, worked twelve-hour shifts, and some were bound for years under contract labour bosses. Alongside the factories were thousands of small workshops tucked into lane houses: machine shops, tailors, weavers, printers and toymakers, often a master, a few journeymen and apprentices working, eating and sleeping in the same rooms. Strikes were frequent, most famously in 1925.",
