    title: "Coal mines at Kailuan, Fushun and Anyuan",
    text: "Modern mining in Republican China meant coal, and foreign capital. The Kailuan mines near Tangshan, run by an Anglo-Chinese company, and Fushun in Manchuria, run by the Japanese South Manchuria Railway, were among the largest in Asia. Miners, recruited from poor villages by labour contractors who took a cut of their wages, worked long shifts underground with picks and baskets, often with little timbering; explosions and flooding killed hundreds at a time. Boys worked hauling coal. At Anyuan in Jiangxi, a strike of thirteen thousand miners in 1922, organised by young Communists including Liu Shaoqi, won better pay and became a founding legend of the Chinese labour movement.",
