    text: "Shanghai shopping ran from the grand to the tiny. On Nanjing Road, the Sincere and Wing On department stores, founded by Cantonese returnees from Australia in 1917 and 1918, sold imported goods at fixed prices with roof gardens and hotels above. Most people shopped at covered municipal markets or street stalls in the early morning, haggling over fish, pork, vegetables and bean curd, and bought hot breakfast from hawkers. Peddlers walked the lanes calling their wares. Money itself was a muddle of silver dollars, copper coins and bank notes until the currency reform of 1935, and in wartime inflation after 1937 people bartered rice, cloth and cigarettes when notes lost value.",
