    text: "The Taiho and Yoro codes, modelled on the Tang, gave Japan a written penal law of five punishments, from beating with a light stick to exile and death, with penalties for brawling graded by weapon and injury, and higher penalties for striking a superior or a parent. Disputes between villagers were heard first by the village head and the district chief, a local magnate who often knew both families; only serious cases went to the provincial governor. Rank mattered: nobles could reduce penalties by forfeiting rank or paying copper. In practice, many quarrels over water, boundaries and debts were settled by the heads of households and village elders, as they would be for centuries after.",
