    text: "Early Joseon inheritance divided property among sons and daughters equally, and a married couple often lived near the wife's family. By the late seventeenth century, Neo-Confucian rules had transformed this: the eldest son inherited the ancestral rites and most of the land, daughters were cut out, and a wife moved into her husband's household for good. A childless couple's future therefore depended on an heir to perform the ancestral rites. If no son was born, a boy of the same lineage and the right generation, often a nephew, was formally adopted with the state's approval, and adoption records became one of the most common documents of late Joseon.",
